Your own periodic jobs, one per line, started at login and reaped with it.

    NAME  TIMESPEC...  --  COMMAND...

The shape is /etc/kdos/timers.d's, and TIMESPEC is `snooze`'s own options —
`-H 9 -M 0` for nine in the morning, `-d /2` for every second day, `-s 4h` for
"run it once on the next login if the machine was off". `snooze --help` is the
whole reference; nothing is invented here.

THE COMMAND IS AN ARGUMENT VECTOR. No pipe, no redirection, no `&&`: a program
that writes a file takes a flag naming it. That is what lets this file be
parsed instead of sourced, and it is why a line here cannot run something you
did not write.

THESE DIE WITH THE SESSION, on purpose. A job that writes into your home has no
business outliving the login that started it, and one that carried on would go
on firing on a machine you had walked away from. The system table at
/etc/kdos/timers.d is the place for a job that must run whether or not anybody
is logged in.

An example, commented out because a table with a job in it is a decision:

  # weather  -H 7 -M 30 -s 2h  --  kdos-notify --send "Good morning"
